name Ankhmaa
// Priority 6, NOT 4. This number is a language PRIORITY and LOWER wins, so at 4
// this file outranked `mn` (no number, so it defaults to 5) for every client that
// selects by language code rather than by filename. Measured through the API:
//
//   espeak_SetVoiceByProperties("mn")        -> Ankhmaa   xgn/mn-f
//   espeak_SetVoiceByProperties("mn", MALE)  -> Ankhmaa   xgn/mn-f
//   espeak_SetVoiceByName("mn")              -> Mongolian xgn/mn
//
// Only the by-name path reached the male voice, because it short-circuits on an
// exact filename match before scoring runs -- and that is the CLI's `-v mn`,
// which is the path every test and every acoustic measurement in this project
// uses. So the tuning was all being validated on a voice that Android TTS, NVDA,
// speech-dispatcher and the language bindings never selected.
//
// `mn` is the voice fitted against a real corpus (one male speaker, 1923
// utterances). mn-f cannot be fitted at all, because no female Mongolian corpus
// exists. The measured voice is the one that should answer to the bare language
// code. mn-f stays fully reachable by name: `-v mn-f`, or SetVoiceByName.
//
// Note this file also cannot declare `gender` -- voices.c rejects a gender
// attribute on a language file -- which is why even a FEMALE request matched it.
language mn 6

// Base/range, not min/max -- see the note in the `mn` voice. Left at the
// original, tested value, deliberately: there is no female Mongolian corpus to
// fit against (mbspeech and fleurs-mn are both male-only), so nothing here can
// be measured, and an unmeasured change is not an improvement.
//
// Two alternatives were tried and reverted. `pitch 120 300` measures a median
// of 287 Hz, far too high for a female voice. `pitch 110 215` widened the range
// (3.22 -> 4.77 st) at 204 Hz, but it also drops formant_factor from 1.177 to
// 1.085, and that factor is doing real work here: it scales the consonant
// transition loci, and a female voice legitimately wants them raised. Lowering
// it partly undoes the formant scaling below, on no evidence.
//
// So the 0.96..1.04 formant_factor rule stated in the `mn` voice does NOT apply
// to this one -- a high base is correct for a female voice. Fitting this
// properly needs a Mongolian corpus containing female speech.
pitch 140 210

speed 85

// Unvoiced consonant amplitude. `mn` sets 115; this file set nothing and so ran
// on espeak's default of 90. A language file inherits nothing from another
// language file -- LoadVoice resets and then applies only this file's lines --
// so the consonant fix landed on `mn` never reached the female voice.
//
// Measured per-phoneme against `mn` over the same sentences, every one of the 14
// WAV-driven unvoiced consonants sat 4.11 dB down (p, t, k, th, q, s, S, x, X,
// ts, tsh, tS, tSh, l#), while voiced segments were within 1.5 dB. That is the
// band carrying the s/sh/ts/ch/x place cues, on the voice most clients select.
//
// Swept against mn: 130 -> -0.98 dB, 145 -> -0.01 dB, 160 -> +0.85 dB.
consonants 145

// Female formant scaling. These ratios are consistent with the general
// male-to-female scaling reported in the phonetics literature (~+15% on
// F1-F3), but they are NOT measured from Mongolian: an earlier comment here
// claimed they came from "acoustic analysis of Ankhmaa recordings vs Ganbaa",
// and no such measurement is supported by anything in this repository.
//
// They could not be verified either. Both corpora available for Mongolian are
// male-only: mbspeech is a single male speaker (median F0 102 Hz), and
// fleurs-mn's test and validation splits measure 119-125 Hz median with no
// clip above 180 Hz. Deriving real ratios needs a Mongolian corpus with
// female speakers.
formant 0 100 100 100
formant 1 112 100 100
formant 2 117 100 100
formant 3 111 100 100

breath 2  3  1  1  1  1
breathw 100 100 100 100 100 100

// Khalkha-specific contours; see phsource/intonation.
// Statement / comma / question / exclamation.
tunes mn_s mn_c mn_q mn_e
